Sure, but spoilers ahoy!

Lichen and lizard corpses do not rot, and can be carried around for
emergency food. Lizard corpses also have the extremely useful effect
of curing delayed-stoning attacks by cockatrices and chickatrices
("You are slowing down", "Your limbs are stiffening"); lizards start
showing up about the time cockatrices and chickatrices do, so when you
see a lizard kill it, keep your pet from eating it and save that
corpse!

There are many resistances and abilities conferred by corpses; one of
the most useful is poison resistance. The most common corpses that
confer poison resistance are themselves poisonous, and eating
poisonous corpses causes damage (which you will heal from if you
survive) and lowering strength (which persists until you use one of
the two or three cures), so the trick is to know which non-poisonous
corpses can confer poison resistance, how to eat poisonous corpses
without being affected, and how to cure the strength loss caused by
poisoning if you can't do either of the former. Lots of spoilery info
here:
http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Poison_resistance

Corpse-conferred abilities that you probably do *not* want right away,
but can come in handy later (you can look at the spoiler sites I
mentioned in a previous post if you want to know which confers
which):
* Teleportitis - useful only once you have teleport control
* Invisibility
* Polymorphing - useful only once you have polymorph control

Corpse-conferred abilities that you probably *never* want:
* Aggravate monster
* Hallucination - has one odd, rare use that has much better
alternatives
* Lycanthropy
* Stunning
* Food poisoning - fatal unless dealt with immediately!

And then there are a few "just don't eat this ever" corpses...

There are also lots of useful abilities conferred by corpses: poison
resistance, fire resistance, cold resistance, shock resistance, sleep
resistance, disintegration resistance, telepathy, teleport control,
energy gain, strength gain, intelligence gain, level gain, intrinsic
speed (but this only works once), healing hitpoints (but this is not
usually a good idea for humans).

Also, a tinning kit is a great thing to have when planning on eating
unhealthy monsters. I'll let you read around for why, if you care to
do so.


